=====================================================================
FINDINGS (facts for the lead; none of these is a K1-K6 failure)
=====================================================================
F1 (CONFIRMS checker-second's FAIL; already conceded as KD-2026-08-30c).
I reproduced their decisive probe independently in my own scratch copy.
Fixture: [{"2025-01-15","Health Insurance Autopay Reversal","150","Health
Insurance"}, {"2025-01-05","Rent","-500","Housing"}] — i.e. an HI history
with no NEGATIVE premium, so metrics.HealthcareCoverageStart returns
hasCoverage=false and ClippedHealthcareMonths==0.
GET /dashboard/kpi/healthcare?start=2025-01-01&end=2025-01-31 rendered:
  TILE Total         = $150.00
  TILE Per Month     = $0.00
  TILE Low (2025-01) = $150.00
  TILE High (2025-01)= $150.00
  ROW cell = $150.00        ROW vs-Avg = 0.0%
The row/Low/High figures are category-gated while Per Month is
coverage-gated, so they diverge inside one modal. Their factual premise is
correct. NOTE for adjudication: this does NOT violate K3 as written — K3
specifies "a fixture with coverageStart INSIDE the range", and on the
no-coverage path Per Month still equals Metrics.HealthcareActual (which is
0.0). It is a Design-point-4 coherence defect, not a K1-K6 miss.

F2 (NEW — not raised by either sibling checker; a regression this attempt
introduces on a surface it creates). kpi-detail.html:9 renders
`<button onclick="exportKPIToCSV('{{.Type}}')">Export CSV</button>` for
EVERY kind, so the two new modals ship a working-looking export button.
handleKPIExport (handlers.go) switches on kpiType with cases for only
income/expenses/savings/savings-rate and no default, so for the new kinds it
writes neither header nor rows. Proven by throwaway probe through the real
router:
  kind=expenses   status=200 bodyLen=31 body="Month,Expenses\n2025-01,2200.00\n"
  kind=living     status=200 bodyLen=0  body=""
  kind=healthcare status=200 bodyLen=0  body=""
A user clicking Export CSV in either new modal downloads a zero-byte file.
This is NOT pre-existing on master (the kinds do not exist there; both cards
opened the expenses modal, whose export works). No numbered criterion covers
export — K6's text is satisfied (no duplicated exclusion logic, no literal) —
so I record it as a finding, not a K6 failure. Recommend folding it into the
attempt-2 brief.

F3 (pre-existing, out of scope, for the backlog). A THIRD card is wired to
the wrong kind: web/templates/components/kpis.html:141, the "Budget" card
(combined Living + Healthcare cumulative variance), still carries
onclick="openKPIDetail('expenses')". Identical on master (git diff shows only
two onclick changes), and the spec's K1 names only the Living and Healthcare
cards. Same defect class the task fixes, one card further on.

F4 (test-quality observation, non-blocking). The two Per-Month tests use
extractDollarAfter(...) with `math.Abs(got-want) > 0.01`, i.e. they parse the
rendered dollar string and then allow one cent of slack, rather than asserting
strict equality against formatMoney(...). I closed that gap myself for K3
(strict string equality, "$405.83" == formatMoney(405.833333), see K3 above)
and for K2 via the render dump ("$1,276.41" vs ActualMonthly=1276.411290).
Both hold strictly today; a strict assertion would be a stronger guard.
Per the V3 "promote the probe" pattern this is worth a test-only follow-up.
